 deferred transaction may start by reading and later try to upgrade to a writer\\. If another connection already prevents that upgrade, SQLite can return `SQLI...\nWAL mode improves reader/writer overlap but preserves SQLite’s single-writer rule\\. The durable fix is short explicit write transactions, a bounded per-connect...\nThis source extract is available as evidence for the active task.\nCoverage: signature, parameter, behavior, example\nMissing: version\nCoverage describes this extract only; it does not establish completion of the active task or independently verify the source's claims.\n\nSource excerpt (untrusted page content):\nDBA PARK may earn a commission from purchases through links in this article, at no extra cost to you\\.\n\nSQLite returns `SQLITE_BUSY`—often shown as “database is locked”—when a connection cannot obtain the lock it needs\\. The fix is rarely “increase the timeout and forget it\\.” Long transactions, a read-to-write upgrade, forgotten cursors, multiple writers, checkpoint pressure, and unsuitable filesystems can all create the same symptom\\.\n\n**What You’ll Learn:** understand SQLite locking, enable WAL mode correctly, set a bounded busy timeout, find long writers, avoid transaction-upgrade deadlocks, and decide when a client/server database is the better architecture\\.\n\n![SQLite WAL reader and single-writer concurrency](https://dbapark.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/sqlite-wal-database-locked.png)WAL lets readers overlap a writer, but there is still only one writer at a time\\.\n\n## 1\\. What WAL Changes—and What It Does Not\n\n\n| Behavior | Rollback journal | WAL |\n| -------- | ---------------- | --- |\n| Readers during a writer | More reader/writer blocking | Readers can usually continue from a snapshot\\. |\n| Number of writers | One | One\\. |\n| Write location | Rollback journal \\+ database | Append to -wal, later checkpoint to database\\. |\n| Extra files | -journal | -wal and -shm\\. |\n| Network filesystem fit | Depends on VFS/locking | WAL requires shared-memory semantics; normally keep it on local storage\\. |\n\n## 2\\. Inspect the Current Database\n\n\n```\nPRAGMA journal_mode;\nPRAGMA busy_timeout;\nPRAGMA synchronous;\nPRAGMA wal_autocheckpoint;\nPRAGMA database_list;\n```\n\nRecord the database path and confirm every process is opening the same file\\. Relative paths can create multiple databases and lead to misleading diagnostics\\.\n\n## 3\\. Enable WAL Mode Once\n\n\n```\nPRAGMA journal_mode = WAL;\nPRAGMA synchronous = NORMAL;\nPRAGMA busy_timeout = 5000;\n```\n\n`journal_mode=WAL` is persistent for the database\\. `busy_timeout` is connection-specific, so set it on every newly opened connection or through the client library configuration\\. Choose a bounded value that matches request latency objectives\\.\n\n> Do not switch a live production database to WAL without testing backup, checkpoint, filesystem, and multi-process behavior\\. Preserve the database, -wal, and -shm relationship during supported backup operations\\.\n\n## 4\\. Keep Write Transactions Short\n\n\n```\n-- Acquire the write reservation before doing work.\nBEGIN IMMEDIATE;\nUPDATE jobs\nSET status = 'running', started_at = CURRENT_TIMESTAMP\nWHERE job_id = 42 AND status = 'queued';\nCOMMIT;\n```\n\n- Do not perform network calls, user interaction, file processing, or model inference inside a write transaction\\.\n- Prepare values before `BEGIN`\\.\n- Write the minimum rows and commit immediately\\.\n- Always roll back in error paths\\.\n- Close cursors/statements so read transactions can finish\\.\n\n## 5\\. Why BEGIN IMMEDIATE Can Help\n\nA deferred transaction may start by reading and later try to upgrade to a writer\\. If another connection already prevents that upgrade, SQLite can return `SQLITE_BUSY` immediately because waiting cannot resolve the lock cycle\\. `BEGIN IMMEDIATE` asks for the write reservation at the start, making contention visible before application work is performed\\.\n\n## 6\\. Configure the Application Connection\n\n\n```\nimport sqlite3\n\ncon = sqlite3.connect(\"app.db\", timeout=5.0, isolation_level=None)\ncon.execute(\"PRAGMA journal_mode=WAL\")\ncon.execute(\"PRAGMA synchronous=NORMAL\")\ncon.execute(\"PRAGMA busy_timeout=5000\")\n\ntry:\n    con.execute(\"BEGIN IMMEDIATE\")\n    con.execute(\n        \"UPDATE jobs SET status=? WHERE job_id=?\",\n        (\"running\", 42),\n    )\n    con.execute(\"COMMIT\")\nexcept Exception:\n    con.execute(\"ROLLBACK\")\n    raise\nfinally:\n    con.close()\n```\n\nThe Python `timeout` and SQLite `busy_timeout` both express waiting behavior; keep the application policy explicit and test the actual driver\\. Add bounded retry with jitter only around idempotent or transactionally safe operations\\.\n\n## 7\\. Inspect and Control Checkpoints\n\n\n```\nPRAGMA wal_checkpoint(PASSIVE);\nPRAGMA wal_checkpoint(RESTART);\nPRAGMA wal_autocheckpoint;\n```\n\nA long-lived reader can prevent old WAL frames from being checkpointed, allowing the WAL file to grow\\. Use `PASSIVE` for observation with minimal interference\\. Schedule more aggressive checkpoint modes only with evidence and an understanding of concurrent readers\\.\n\n## 8\\. A Reproducible Contention Test\n\n1. Connection A runs `BEGIN IMMEDIATE` and updates a row without committing\\.\n1. Connection B sets `busy_timeout=2000` and attempts `BEGIN IMMEDIATE`\\.\n1. Verify that B waits about two seconds and returns busy\\.\n1. Commit A and repeat; B should now succeed\\.\n1. Move artificial work outside A’s transaction and compare lock duration\\.\n\n## 9\\. Root-Cause Checklist\n\n- Which process owns the current writer?\n- How long is its transaction open?\n- Is a cursor keeping a read transaction alive?\n- Does every connection set busy\\_timeout?\n- Is the application upgrading a deferred read transaction to a write?\n- Are multiple worker processes writing concurrently without serialization?\n- Is WAL growing because checkpoints cannot advance?\n- Is the database on a network share, sync folder, or unsupported VFS?\n- Could a crashed process, antivirus, or backup tool be interfering with files?\n\n## 10\\. When SQLite Is No Longer the Right Fit\n\nSQLite is excellent for embedded applications, desktop tools, mobile devices, local services, tests, and modest single-writer workloads\\. Move to PostgreSQL, SQL Server, MySQL, or another server database when sustained concurrent writes, remote access, centralized administration, high availability, or independent scaling are core requirements\\.\n\n## Summary\n\nWAL mode improves reader/writer overlap but preserves SQLite’s single-writer rule\\. The durable fix is short explicit write transactions, a bounded per-connection timeout, correct checkpoint and filesystem behavior, and writer serialization where necessary\\. A larger timeout alone only hides the queue\\.\n\n**Continue learning:** To strengthen your SQLite query-writing foundation after resolving contention, [browse Udemy](https://trk.udemy.com/jRGNga) and search for SQLite SQL courses with exercises on queries, updates, and application database access\\. To strengthen the SQL querying and relational database fundamentals behind your work, explore the interactive courses on [DataCamp](https://datacamp.pxf.io/4aAamM)\\.\n\n## Related DBA park Guides\n\n- [SQLite vs DuckDB](https://dbapark.com/sqlite-vs-duckdb-embedded-database/)\n\n## Official References\n\n- [SQLite documentation: Write-Ahead Logging](https://sqlite.org/wal.html)\n- [SQLite documentation: PRAGMA statements](https://sqlite.org/pragma.html)\n- [SQLite documentation: Result and error codes](https://sqlite.org/rescode.html)","type":"text"}],"details":{"authoritative":false,"cacheHit":false,"compressionMode":"normal","contentType":"text/html; charset=UTF-8","coverage":{"kind":"docs","missing":["version"],"present":["signature","parameter","behavior","example"],"score":4},"coverageKind":"docs","excerptChars":6822,"extractedChars":6822,"extractionMethod":"dom-markdown-v1","facts":["SQLite returns `SQLITE_BUSY`—often shown as “database is locked”—when a connection cannot obtain the lock it needs\\. The fix is rarely “increase the timeout an...","Is WAL growing because checkpoints cannot advance?","**What You’ll Learn:** understand SQLite locking, enable WAL mode correctly, set a bounded busy timeout, find long writers, avoid transaction-upgrade deadlocks...","![SQLite WAL reader and single-writer concurrency](https://dbapark.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/sqlite-wal-database-locked.png)WAL lets readers overlap a wri..."],"finalUrl":"https://dbapark.com/sqlite-database-is-locked-wal-fix/","intent":"docs_reference","metaDescription":"Diagnose and fix SQLITE_BUSY and “database is locked” with short transactions, WAL mode, busy_timeout, checkpoints, and ...","networkEvidence":{"artifactId":"art_562297c3ce353043e3a175f42f0552e5","bodySha256":"685099946e3f8ee4f34639767995ea9ffe722e0a0c0b8df4294a8b19075d8eab","redirectChain":[],"trust":"untrusted"},"preview":"SQLite returns `SQLITE_BUSY`—often shown as “database is locked”—when a connection cannot obtain the lock it needs\\. The fix is rarely “increase the timeout and forget it\\.” Long ...\n**What You’ll Learn:** understand SQLite locking, enable WAL mode correctly, set a bounded busy timeout, find long writers, avoid transaction-upgrade deadlocks, and decide when a ...\nA deferred transaction may start by reading and later try to upgrade to a writer\\. If another connection already prevents that upgrade, SQLite can return `SQLITE_BUSY` immediately...\n- Is WAL growing because checkpoints cannot advance?\nWAL mode improves reader/writer overlap but preserves SQLite’s single-writer rule\\. The durable fix is short explicit write transactions, a bounded per-connection timeout, correct...\n![SQLite WAL reader and single-writer concurrency](https://dbapark.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/sqlite-wal-database-locked.png)WAL lets readers overlap a writer, but there is st...\n| Write location | Rollback journal \\+ database | Append to -wal, later checkpoint to database\\. |\nPRAGMA wal_autocheckpoint;\n## 3\\. Enable WAL Mode Once\n> Do not switch a live production database to WAL without testing backup, checkpoint, filesystem, and multi-process behavior\\. Preserve the database, -wal, and -shm relationship d...\nPRAGMA wal_checkpoint(PASSIVE);\nPRAGMA wal_checkpoint(RESTART);","status":"rich","statusCode":200,"title":"SQLite “Database Is Locked”: WAL Mode, busy_timeout, and a Reliable Fix","truncated":false,"url":"https://dbapark.com/sqlite-database-is-locked-wal-fix/"}},"search":{"content":[{"text":"Web search: SQLite WAL checkpoint SQLITE_BUSY official documentation\nSearch strategy: documentation query biased toward official reference pages\nTop sources:\n1. SQLite \"Database Is Locked\": WAL Mode, busy_timeout, and a Reliable Fix — https://dbapark.com/sqlite-database-is-locked-wal-fix/\n   SQLite returns SQLITE_BUSY —often shown as \"database is locked\"—when a connection cannot obtain the lock it needs. The fix is rarely \"increase the timeout and forget it.\" Long transactions, a read-to-write upgrade, forgotten cursors, multiple writers, checkpoint pressure, and unsuitable filesystems can all create the ...\n2. Checkpoint a database - SQLite Documentation - TypeError [authoritative] — https://typeerror.org/docs/sqlite/c3ref/wal_checkpoint_v2\n   The sqlite3_wal_checkpoint_v2 (D,X,M,L,C) interface runs a checkpoint operation on database X of database connection D in mode M. Status information is written back into integers pointed to by L and C.\n3. Checkpoint a database - SQLite Documentation - TypeError [authoritative] — https://www.typeerror.org/docs/sqlite/c3ref/wal_checkpoint\n   In brief, sqlite3_wal_checkpoint (D,X) causes the content in the write-ahead log for database X on database connection D to be transferred into the database file and for the write-ahead log to be reset. See the checkpointing documentation for addition information.\n4. Checkpoint a Database - SQLite - W3cubDocs [authoritative] — https://docs.w3cub.com/sqlite/c3ref/wal_checkpoint_v2.html\n   The sqlite3_wal_checkpoint_v2 (D,X,M,L,C) interface runs a checkpoint operation on database X of database connection D in mode M. Status information is written back into integers pointed to by L and C.\nNext step: webFetch https://dbapark.com/sqlite-database-is-locked-wal-fix/","type":"text"}],"details":{"avoidedDomains":["medium.com","dev.to","reddit.com","stackoverflow.com"],"cacheAgeMs":0,"cacheHit":false,"candidateCount":10,"compressionMode":"normal","effectiveQuery":"SQLite WAL checkpoint SQLITE_BUSY official documentation","fallbackUsed":false,"followUpQueries":["SQLite WAL checkpoint SQLITE_BUSY official documentation official docs","SQLite WAL checkpoint SQLITE_BUSY official documentation reference"],"freshness":"any","intent":"docs_reference","networkEvidence":{"artifactIds":["art_ca943706de8702464c312c156816c7e0","art_a1255b7f782e28c09bcfe6c57495cf7e"],"fallbackUrlsFetched":false,"trust":"untrusted"},"preferCorroboration":false,"preferredSourceUrls":["https://dbapark.com/sqlite-database-is-locked-wal-fix/","https://typeerror.org/docs/sqlite/c3ref/wal_checkpoint_v2","https://www.typeerror.org/docs/sqlite/c3ref/wal_checkpoint","https://docs.w3cub.com/sqlite/c3ref/wal_checkpoint_v2.html"],"probeElapsedMs":952,"query":"SQLite WAL checkpoint SQLITE_BUSY official documentation","resultCount":4,"results":[{"authoritative":false,"fetchRisk":"medium","score":37,"scoreReasons":["query_term_match","topical_terms:4/4"],"snippet":"SQLite returns SQLITE_BUSY —often shown as \"database is locked\"—when a connection cannot obtain the lock it needs. The fix is rarely \"increase the timeout and forget it.\" Long transactions, a read-to-write upgrade, forgotten cursors, multiple writers, checkpoint pressure, and unsuitable filesystems can all create the same symptom.","sourceKind":"general","title":"SQLite \"Database Is Locked\": WAL Mode, busy_timeout, and a Reliable Fix","url":"https://dbapark.com/sqlite-database-is-locked-wal-fix/"},{"authoritative":true,"fetchRisk":"low","score":35,"scoreReasons":["docs_path","query_term_match","topical_terms:3/4"],"snippet":"The sqlite3_wal_checkpoint_v2 (D,X,M,L,C) interface runs a checkpoint operation on database X of database connection D in mode M. Status information is written back into integers pointed to by L and C.","sourceKind":"docs","title":"Checkpoint a database - SQLite Documentation - TypeError","url":"https://typeerror.org/docs/sqlite/c3ref/wal_checkpoint_v2"},{"authoritative":true,"fetchRisk":"low","score":35,"scoreReasons":["docs_path","query_term_match","topical_terms:3/4"],"snippet":"In brief, sqlite3_wal_checkpoint (D,X) causes the content in the write-ahead log for database X on database connection D to be transferred into the database file and for the write-ahead log to be reset. See the checkpointing documentation for addition information.","sourceKind":"docs","title":"Checkpoint a database - SQLite Documentation - TypeError","url":"https://www.typeerror.org/docs/sqlite/c3ref/wal_checkpoint"},{"authoritative":true,"fetchRisk":"low","score":33,"scoreReasons":["docs_path","query_term_match","topical_terms:3/4"],"snippet":"The sqlite3_wal_checkpoint_v2 (D,X,M,L,C) interface runs a checkpoint operation on database X of database connection D in mode M. Status information is written back into integers pointed to by L and C.","sourceKind":"docs","title":"Checkpoint a Database - SQLite - W3cubDocs","url":"https://docs.w3cub.com/sqlite/c3ref/wal_checkpoint_v2.html"}],"searchAttempts":[{"provider":"duckduckgo-web","resultCount":10,"status":"ok","url":"https://noai.duckduckgo.com/?q=SQLite%20WAL%20checkpoint%20SQLITE_BUSY%20official%20documentation"}],"searchProvider":"duckduckgo-web","searchProviderMode":"auto","searchProviderOrder":["duckduckgo"],"searchStrategy":"documentation query biased toward official reference pages","selectionPolicy":"relevance-source-version-v1","sourceUrls":["https://dbapark.com/sqlite-database-is-locked-wal-fix/","https://typeerror.org/docs/sqlite/c3ref/wal_checkpoint_v2","https://www.typeerror.org/docs/sqlite/c3ref/wal_checkpoint","https://docs.w3cub.com/sqlite/c3ref/wal_checkpoint_v2.html"]}}},{"fetch":{"content":[{"text":"Fetched source: https://doc.rust-lang.org/std/iter/index.html\nTitle: std::iter - Rust\nKey facts:\n- If you’re creating a collection, implementing [IntoIterator](https://doc.rust-lang.org/std/iter/trait.IntoIterator.html \"trait std::iter::IntoIterator\") for it...\n- An iterator has a method, [next](https://doc.rust-lang.org/std/iter/trait.Iterator.html#tymethod.next \"method std::iter::Iterator::next\"), which when called, r...\n- If you’re writing an [Iterator](https://doc.rust-lang.org/std/iter/trait.Iterator.html \"trait std::iter::Iterator\"), you can use it with a `for` loop\\.\n- If a collection type `C` provides `iter()`, it usually also implements `IntoIterator` for `&C`, with an implementation that just calls `iter()`\\. Likewise, a c...\nKey excerpt:\nIf you’ve found yourself with a collection of some kind, and needed to perform an operation on the elements of said collection, you’ll quickly run into ‘iterat...\n1. If you’re creating a collection, implementing [IntoIterator](https://doc.rust-lang.org/std/iter/trait.IntoIterator.html \"trait std::iter::IntoIterator\") for...\n[DoubleEndedIterator](https://doc.rust-lang.org/std/iter/trait.DoubleEndedIterator.html \"trait std::iter::DoubleEndedIterator\")An iterator able to yield elemen...\n- [Structs](https://doc.rust-lang.org/std/iter/index.html#structs) are often the return types of the various methods on this module’s traits\\. You’ll usually w...\nThis extract is from an authoritative documentation page; its applicability depends on the active task and version.\nCoverage: signature, parameter, behavior, example, version\nCoverage describes this extract only; it does not establish completion of the active task or independently verify the source's claims.\n\nSource excerpt (untrusted page content):\n\n[Source passage omitted]\n\nIf you’ve found yourself with a collection of some kind, and needed to perform an operation on the elements of said collection, you’ll quickly run into ‘iterators’\\. Iterators are heavily used in idiomatic Rust code, so it’s worth becoming familiar with them\\.\n\n\n[Source passage omitted]\n\n## [§](https://doc.rust-lang.org/std/iter/index.html#iterator)Iterator\n\nThe heart and soul of this module is the [Iterator](https://doc.rust-lang.org/std/iter/trait.Iterator.html \"trait std::iter::Iterator\") trait\\. The core of [Iterator](https://doc.rust-lang.org/std/iter/trait.Iterator.html \"trait std::iter::Iterator\") looks like this:\n\n\n[Source passage omitted]\n\nAn iterator has a method, [next](https://doc.rust-lang.org/std/iter/trait.Iterator.html#tymethod.next \"method std::iter::Iterator::next\"), which when called, returns an `[Option](https://doc.rust-lang.org/std/option/enum.Option.html \"enum std::option::Option\")<Item>`\\. Calling [next](https://doc.rust-lang.org/std/iter/trait.Iterator.html#tymethod.next \"method std::iter::Iterator::next\") will return [Some\\(Item\\)](https://doc.rust-lang.org/std/option/enum.Option.html#variant.Some \"variant std::option::Option::Some\") as long as there are elements, and once they’ve all been exhausted, will return `None` to indicate that iteration is finished\\. Individual iterators may choose to resume iteration, and so calling [next](https://doc.rust-lang.org/std/iter/trait.Iterator.html#tymethod.next \"method std::iter::Iterator::next\") again may or may not eventually start returning [Some\\(Item\\)](https://doc.rust-lang.org/std/option/enum.Option.html#variant.Some \"variant std::option::Option::Some\") again at some point \\(for example, see [TryIter](https://doc.rust-lang.org/std/sync/mpsc/struct.TryIter.html)\\)\\.\n\n[Iterator](https://doc.rust-lang.org/std/iter/trait.Iterator.html \"trait std::iter::Iterator\")’s full definition includes a number of other methods as well, but they are default methods, built on top of [next](https://doc.rust-lang.org/std/iter/trait.Iterator.html#tymethod.next \"method std::iter::Iterator::next\"), and so you get them for free\\.\n\nIterators are also composable, and it’s common to chain them together to do more complex forms of processing\\. See the [Adapters](https://doc.rust-lang.org/std/iter/index.html#adapters) section below for more details\\.\n\n\n[Source passage omitted]\n\nThere are three common methods which can create iterators from a collection:\n\n\n[Source passage omitted]\n\n## [§](https://doc.rust-lang.org/std/iter/index.html#implementing-iterator)Implementing Iterator\n\nCreating an iterator of your own involves two steps: creating a `struct` to hold the iterator’s state, and then implementing [Iterator](https://doc.rust-lang.org/std/iter/trait.Iterator.html \"trait std::iter::Iterator\") for that `struct`\\. This is why there are so many `struct`s in this module: there is one for each iterator and iterator adapter\\.\n\n\n[Source passage omitted]\n\nCalling [next](https://doc.rust-lang.org/std/iter/trait.Iterator.html#tymethod.next \"method std::iter::Iterator::next\") this way gets repetitive\\. Rust has a construct which can call [next](https://doc.rust-lang.org/std/iter/trait.Iterator.html#tymethod.next \"method std::iter::Iterator::next\") on your iterator, until it reaches `None`\\. Let’s go over that next\\.\n\n\n[Source passage omitted]\n\n## [§](https://doc.rust-lang.org/std/iter/index.html#for-loops-and-intoiterator)`for` loops and `IntoIterator`\n\nRust’s `for` loop syntax is actually sugar for iterators\\. Here’s a basic example of `for`:\n\n\n[Source passage omitted]\n\nThere’s a trait in the standard library for converting something into an iterator: [IntoIterator](https://doc.rust-lang.org/std/iter/trait.IntoIterator.html \"trait std::iter::IntoIterator\")\\. This trait has one method, [into\\_iter](https://doc.rust-lang.org/std/iter/trait.IntoIterator.html#tymethod.into_iter \"method std::iter::IntoIterator::into\\_iter\"), which converts the thing implementing [IntoIterator](https://doc.rust-lang.org/std/iter/trait.IntoIterator.html \"trait std::iter::IntoIterator\") into an iterator\\. Let’s take a look at that `for` loop again, and what the compiler converts it into:\n\n\n[Source passage omitted]\n\nRust de-sugars this into:\n\n\n[Source passage omitted]\n\nFirst, we call `into_iter()` on the value\\. Then, we match on the iterator that returns, calling [next](https://doc.rust-lang.org/std/iter/trait.Iterator.html#tymethod.next \"method std::iter::Iterator::next\") over and over until we see a `None`\\. At that point, we `break` out of the loop, and we’re done iterating\\.\n\nThere’s one more subtle bit here: the standard library contains an interesting implementation of [IntoIterator](https://doc.rust-lang.org/std/iter/trait.IntoIterator.html \"trait std::iter::IntoIterator\"):\n\n[ⓘ](https://doc.rust-lang.org/std/iter/index.html# \"This example is not tested\")\n```\nimpl<I: Iterator> IntoIterator for I\n```\n\nIn other words, all [Iterator](https://doc.rust-lang.org/std/iter/trait.Iterator.html \"trait std::iter::Iterator\")s implement [IntoIterator](https://doc.rust-lang.org/std/iter/trait.IntoIterator.html \"trait std::iter::IntoIterator\"), by just returning themselves\\. This means two things:\n\n1. If you’re writing an [Iterator](https://doc.rust-lang.org/std/iter/trait.Iterator.html \"trait std::iter::Iterator\"), you can use it with a `for` loop\\.\n1. If you’re creating a collection, implementing [IntoIterator](https://doc.rust-lang.org/std/iter/trait.IntoIterator.html \"trait std::iter::IntoIterator\") for it will allow your collection to be used with the `for` loop\\.\n\n## [§](https://doc.rust-lang.org/std/iter/index.html#iterating-by-reference)Iterating by reference\n\nSince [into\\_iter\\(\\)](https://doc.rust-lang.org/std/iter/trait.IntoIterator.html#tymethod.into_iter \"method std::iter::IntoIterator::into\\_iter\") takes `self` by value, using a `for` loop to iterate over a collection consumes that collection\\. Often, you may want to iterate over a collection without consuming it\\. Many collections offer methods that provide iterators over references, conventionally called `iter()` and `iter_mut()` respectively:\n\n\n[Source passage omitted]\n\nFunctions which take an [Iterator](https://doc.rust-lang.org/std/iter/trait.Iterator.html \"trait std::iter::Iterator\") and return another [Iterator](https://doc.rust-lang.org/std/iter/trait.Iterator.html \"trait std::iter::Iterator\") are often called ‘iterator adapters’, as they’re a form of the ‘adapter pattern’\\.\n\nCommon iterator adapters include [map](https://doc.rust-lang.org/std/iter/trait.Iterator.html#method.map \"method std::iter::Iterator::map\"), [take](https://doc.rust-lang.org/std/iter/trait.Iterator.html#method.take \"method std::iter::Iterator::take\"), and [filter](https://doc.rust-lang.org/std/iter/trait.Iterator.html#method.filter \"method std::iter::Iterator::filter\")\\. For more, see their documentation\\.\n\nIf an iterator adapter panics, the iterator will be in an unspecified \\(but memory safe\\) state\\. This state is also not guaranteed to stay the same across versions of Rust, so you should avoid relying on the exact values returned by an iterator which panicked\\.\n\n\n[Source passage omitted]\n\nIterators \\(and iterator [adapters](https://doc.rust-lang.org/std/iter/index.html#adapters)\\) are *lazy*\\. This means that just creating an iterator doesn’t *do* a whole lot\\. Nothing really happens until you call [next](https://doc.rust-lang.org/std/iter/trait.Iterator.html#tymethod.next \"method std::iter::Iterator::next\")\\. This is sometimes a source of confusion when creating an iterator solely for its side effects\\. For example, the [map](https://doc.rust-lang.org/std/iter/trait.Iterator.html#method.map \"method std::iter::Iterator::map\") method calls a closure on each element it iterates over:\n\n\n[Source passage omitted]\n\nThe idiomatic way to write a [map](https://doc.rust-lang.org/std/iter/trait.Iterator.html#method.map \"method std::iter::Iterator::map\") for its side effects is to use a `for` loop or call the [for\\_each](https://doc.rust-lang.org/std/iter/trait.Iterator.html#method.for_each \"method std::iter::Iterator::for\\_each\") method:\n\n\n[Source passage omitted]\n\nAnother common way to evaluate an iterator is to use the [collect](https://doc.rust-lang.org/std/iter/trait.Iterator.html#method.collect \"method std::iter::Iterator::collect\") method to produce a new collection\\.\n\n## [§](https://doc.rust-lang.org/std/iter/index.html#infinity)Infinity\n\n\n[Source passage omitted]\n\n[iter](https://doc.rust-lang.org/std/iter/macro.iter.html \"macro std::iter::iter\")ExperimentalCreates a new closure that returns an iterator where each iteration steps the given generator to the next `yield` statement\\.\n\n\n[Source passage omitted]\n\n## Traits[§](https://doc.rust-lang.org/std/iter/index.html#traits)\n\n[DoubleEndedIterator](https://doc.rust-lang.org/std/iter/trait.DoubleEndedIterator.html \"trait std::iter::DoubleEndedIterator\")An iterator able to yield elements from both ends\\.[ExactSizeIterator](https://doc.rust-lang.org/std/iter/trait.ExactSizeIterator.html \"trait std::iter::ExactSizeIterator\")An iterator that knows its exact length\\.[Extend](https://doc.rust-lang.org/std/iter/trait.Extend.html \"trait std::iter::Extend\")Extend a collection with the contents of an iterator\\.[FromIterator](https://doc.rust-lang.org/std/iter/trait.FromIterator.html \"trait std::iter::FromIterator\")Conversion from an [Iterator](https://doc.rust-lang.org/std/iter/trait.Iterator.html \"trait std::iter::Iterator\")\\.[FusedIterator](https://doc.rust-lang.org/std/iter/trait.FusedIterator.html \"trait std::iter::FusedIterator\")An iterator that always continues to yield `None` when exhausted\\.[IntoIterator](https://doc.rust-lang.org/std/iter/trait.IntoIterator.html \"trait std::iter::IntoIterator\")Conversion into an [Iterator](https://doc.rust-lang.org/std/iter/trait.Iterator.html \"trait std::iter::Iterator\")\\.[Iterator](https://doc.rust-lang.org/std/iter/trait.Iterator.html \"trait std::iter::Iterator\")A trait for dealing with iterators\\.[Product](https://doc.rust-lang.org/std/iter/trait.Product.html \"trait std::iter::Product\")Trait to represent types that can be created by multiplying elements of an iterator\\.[Sum](https://doc.rust-lang.org/std/iter/trait.Sum.html \"trait std::iter::Sum\")Trait to represent types that can be created by summing up an iterator\\.[Step](https://doc.rust-lang.org/std/iter/trait.Step.html \"trait std::iter::Step\")ExperimentalObjects that have a notion of *successor* and *predecessor* operations\\.[TrustedLen](https://doc.rust-lang.org/std/iter/trait.TrustedLen.html \"trait std::iter::TrustedLen\")ExperimentalAn iterator that reports an accurate length using size\\_hint\\.[TrustedStep](https://doc.rust-lang.org/std/iter/trait.TrustedStep.html \"trait std::iter::TrustedStep\")ExperimentalA type that upholds all invariants of [Step](https://doc.rust-lang.org/std/iter/trait.Step.html \"trait std::iter::Step\")\\.\n\n## Functions[§](https://doc.rust-lang.org/std/iter/index.html#functions)\n\n\n[Excerpt incomplete; other source content omitted. Raw response retained in network evidence.]","type":"text"}],"details":{"authoritative":true,"cacheHit":false,"compressionMode":"normal","contentType":"text/html","coverage":{"kind":"docs","missing":[],"present":["signature","parameter","behavior","example","version"],"score":5},"coverageKind":"docs","excerptChars":11117,"extractedChars":25330,"extractionMethod":"dom-markdown-v1","facts":["If you’re creating a collection, implementing [IntoIterator](https://doc.rust-lang.org/std/iter/trait.IntoIterator.html \"trait 